"""
app_apis.chatwoot_connector -- outbound messaging through Chatwoot.

DELIBERATELY STANDALONE. Nothing in this app imports this module: there is no
hooks.py entry, no doc_events, no scheduler job, no call from the valuation or
IM code. It is reached only when something explicitly asks for it --

    Client Script:  frappe.call("app_apis.chatwoot_connector.send_ticket_message",
                                {ticket: frm.doc.name, template: "valuation"})
    Server Script:  from app_apis.chatwoot_connector import send_ticket_message
    bench:          bench --site <site> execute app_apis.chatwoot_connector.test_connection

-- so adding, breaking or removing this file cannot change the behaviour of
anything already running. That separation is the point; keep it. If a future
hook needs to send a message, the hook calls in here, not the other way round.

It is also OFF until switched on. `chatwoot_enabled` on the `app_apis` settings
doctype defaults to unchecked, so filling in a domain and a token configures the
connection without any message leaving the site.

Architecture
------------
    Client Script / Server Script / bench
          |  frappe.call("app_apis.chatwoot_connector.send_ticket_message", ...)
          v
    THIS MODULE  -- reads settings, renders the ar/en template, resolves the
                    customer's phone, finds-or-creates the Chatwoot contact and
                    conversation, posts the message
          |  HTTPS + `api_access_token` header
          v
    https://<your-chatwoot-domain>/api/v1/accounts/<id>/...

What Chatwoot's shape forces on this module
-------------------------------------------
1. THREE CALLS, NOT ONE. Chatwoot has no "send to this phone number" endpoint.
   A message belongs to a conversation, a conversation belongs to a contact in a
   specific inbox, and a contact has to exist first. `_resolve_conversation`
   walks that chain and reuses whatever already exists, so replying about the
   same ticket twice does not open two threads at the customer.

2. THE INBOX IS NOT OPTIONAL. Which channel the message physically goes out on
   (WhatsApp, SMS, web) is decided entirely by `chatwoot_inbox_id`. Chatwoot
   will happily create a conversation in the wrong inbox and the message will
   silently go nowhere useful, so a missing inbox id is a hard error here.

3. A CONTACT'S source_id IS PER-INBOX. An existing contact that has never been
   messaged on this inbox has no source_id for it, and creating the conversation
   without one fails. `_source_id` mints it on demand.

4. RESOLVED CONVERSATIONS ARE REUSED, NOT REOPENED. Posting into a resolved
   thread quietly reopens it in the agent's queue, which is usually what you
   want for a ticket follow-up and never surprising.

Errors never raise. Every public function returns a dict shaped
`{"ok": bool, ...}` -- the same convention as im_connector -- so a Client Script
can render the failure instead of catching an exception it cannot see.

This module defines no doctypes. It owns only the Chatwoot section of `app_apis`.

Two connectors, one live
------------------------
There are two independent Chatwoot configurations. Connector 1 is the original
above: an agent/admin token talking to Chatwoot's REST API. Connector 2 is an
alternative kept alongside it -- same rendered messages, a different route out --
so a site can try another way of delivering without disturbing the one that
works. `chatwoot_active_connector` (1 or 2) picks which one every send actually
uses; nothing else about the call sites changes. Connector 2 offers two methods:

    agent_bot  -- the same REST chain, but with connector 2's own token. Point
                  it at an Agent Bot or an Administrator token and it can open
                  conversations on any inbox -- the thing a plain agent token,
                  a member of no inbox, cannot do (that 401 is exactly why this
                  second connector exists).

    webhook    -- Chatwoot is not called at all. The message is POSTed to your
                  own URL as JSON, and that endpoint delivers it however it
                  likes. See `_send_via_webhook`.

`active_settings()` is the single place the choice is read; `_settings()` and
`_settings2()` are the two raw sources. Everything downstream is connector-blind.
"""

